\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Adaptation dynamics and the cost--quality tradeoff.}{15}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces ParetoBandit adaptation dynamics under catastrophic model failure: normal $\to $ Mistral failure (reward $\to $ 0.05, cost $\to $ \$0, step\nonbreakingspace 608) $\to $ recovered (step\nonbreakingspace $2{\times }$608). $K{=}3$, 20\nonbreakingspace seeds, 95\% bootstrap CI. \textbf  {(a)}\nonbreakingspace Gemini-Pro selection fraction: the unconstrained baseline freely shifts ${\sim }80\%$ of traffic to expensive Gemini-Pro when Mistral fails, while budget-constrained conditions remain near zero---forced to absorb the failure via cheaper Llama-8B. \textbf  {(b)}\nonbreakingspace Windowed mean reward (trailing 50\nonbreakingspace steps): the Gemini gap in\nonbreakingspace (a) explains the quality gap---unconstrained recovers to ${\sim }0.9$ during failure while constrained conditions dip more because Llama is lower quality. \textbf  {(c)}\nonbreakingspace Windowed average cost per request; dotted lines mark budget targets. All three budget regimes maintain compliance ($0.97$--$1.03\times $) throughout the failure, while unconstrained cost spikes. See Table\nonbreakingspace \ref {tab:failure_response} for quantitative comparisons across algorithms.}}{16}{figure.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:catastrophic_failure}{{5}{16}{ParetoBandit adaptation dynamics under catastrophic model failure: normal $\to $ Mistral failure (reward $\to $ \cfFailureReward , cost $\to $ \$0, step~\cfPhaseN ) $\to $ recovered (step~$2{\times }$\cfPhaseN ). $K{=}3$, \cfNseeds ~seeds, 95\% bootstrap CI. \textbf {(a)}~Gemini-Pro selection fraction: the unconstrained baseline freely shifts ${\sim }80\%$ of traffic to expensive Gemini-Pro when Mistral fails, while budget-constrained conditions remain near zero---forced to absorb the failure via cheaper Llama-8B. \textbf {(b)}~Windowed mean reward (trailing 50~steps): the Gemini gap in~(a) explains the quality gap---unconstrained recovers to ${\sim }0.9$ during failure while constrained conditions dip more because Llama is lower quality. \textbf {(c)}~Windowed average cost per request; dotted lines mark budget targets. All three budget regimes maintain compliance ($0.97$--$1.03\times $) throughout the failure, while unconstrained cost spikes. See Table~\ref {tab:failure_response} for quantitative comparisons across algorithms}{figure.caption.38}{}}
